 "lineas": [
  {
   "texto": "In Japan, a busy road suddenly opened up and swallowed a truck. How does a road just vanish?",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "The answer was a giant sewer pipe, buried under the street.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Sewage gives off a gas that turns into acid, and over decades it eats the concrete.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Once there's a crack, the soil around it falls into the pipe, and the water carries it away.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Grain by grain, a hidden cave grows under the road.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "From above, the asphalt looks perfectly fine. It's just a thin lid over the hole.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Until something heavy rolls over it, and the lid gives way.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "After that, Japan ordered checks of its big old sewer pipes, to find the weak spots first.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "The hole starts years before anyone sees it. Now you know.",
   "pausa": 0.2
  }
 ],
